<!-- ===== SECTION 1: THE VERSIONING PROBLEM ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="data-versioning-problem">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>The Data Versioning Problem: Why Git Isn't Enough</h2>

  <p>Git revolutionized software development by enabling version control, branching, and collaboration. But Git has a critical limitation: it stores complete file contents. When you modify a 500MB dataset and commit it, Git stores another full 500MB copy. Your repository doubles in size. Commit it ten times with small changes, and you have 5GB of bloat.</p>

  <p>This is where Data Version Control (DVC) enters. DVC is built specifically for versioning data, models, and ML pipelines. It uses checksums and pointers instead of storing full file contents. When data changes, DVC detects exactly what changed and stores only that delta. Your Git repository stays clean and lean while your data gets the version control it deserves.</p>

  <!-- ANALOGY BOX -->
  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Git works like a backup system that stores complete snapshots. Every commit is a full backup. DVC works like a deduplication system. When you change a file, DVC stores only the changed parts and uses pointers to unchanged data. It's the difference between backing up an entire office every time one document changes versus just backing up that one document and pointing to the rest.</p>
  </div>

