  <p>Here's the key insight: DVC separates concerns. Git tracks code and DVC metadata (small text files with checksums). Actual data files live in a remote backend — S3, Google Cloud Storage, local NAS, or even HDFS. Your Git repository stays under 100MB while you version terabytes of data.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Bash</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Git approach: add 500MB file directly</span>
<span class="va">git add</span> <span class="st">data/customers.csv</span>       <span class="cm"># Git repository grows by 500MB</span>
<span class="va">git commit</span> <span class="st">-m "Add customer data"</span> <span class="cm"># Another 500MB+ when data changes</span>

<span class="cm"># DVC approach: track file with DVC, metadata in Git</span>
<span class="va">dvc add</span> <span class="st">data/customers.csv</span>      <span class="cm"># Creates customers.csv.dvc (tiny file)</span>
<span class="va">git add</span> <span class="st">data/customers.csv.dvc</span>   <span class="cm"># Add only the checksum metadata</span>
<span class="va">git commit</span> <span class="st">-m "Add customer data"</span> <span class="cm"># Git repo grows by maybe 1KB</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> With Git, you're storing the actual 500MB file in version control. Every change creates another full copy. With DVC, you store only a .dvc file containing the checksum. Git tracks the metadata, DVC tracks the data in a separate backend. Your repository stays lean.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- WHY THIS MATTERS -->
  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>For Data Scientists:</strong> Without DVC, managing data versions is manual: data_v1, data_v2, data_final, data_final_real.csv. This is error-prone and doesn't integrate with Git workflows. DVC brings Git-style workflows to data.</p>
    <p><strong>For Production Systems:</strong> Reproducibility is critical. If a model trained on data from March makes different predictions than one trained on the same logical data from April, you need to know why. DVC tracks exact data versions and enables reproduction.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Git stores full file contents; DVC uses checksums and remote backends</li>
      <li>DVC keeps Git repositories small while enabling complete data versioning</li>
      <li>Metadata lives in Git (.dvc files), actual data in S3/GCS/local storage</li>
      <li>Same collaboration model as Git: push, pull, merge, branch</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- QUIZ -->
  <div class="quiz-box">
    <h4>🧪 Test Your Understanding</h4>
