      <p>Why can't you efficiently version large CSV files with Git?</p>
      <ul class="quiz-options">
        <li>A) Git stores complete file contents; every change means another full copy</li>
        <li>B) Git is designed only for code, not data</li>
        <li>C) Large files automatically fail in Git</li>
        <li>D) Git requires manual versioning of data files</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="quiz-answer">Git stores full file contents by design. When you modify a 500MB CSV and commit, Git stores another complete 500MB copy. This is perfect for code (small changes, frequent commits) but terrible for data (large files, occasional changes). DVC solves this by using checksums and storing data separately.</div>
