<!-- ===== SECTION 2: DVC PIPELINES ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="dvc-pipeline-architecture">
  <span class="section-label">Architecture</span>
  <h2>DVC Pipelines: Reproducible ML Workflows</h2>

  <p>DVC's real power lies in pipeline definitions. Instead of manually running scripts in order, you define stages: data loading, preprocessing, feature engineering, model training. Each stage specifies inputs, outputs, and the command to run. DVC automatically tracks dependencies and can reproduce the entire workflow from scratch.</p>

  <p>This is revolutionary for ML teams. When a data scientist changes a preprocessing script, DVC knows exactly which downstream stages need recomputation. It can skip expensive stages whose dependencies haven't changed. It ensures that if you run the pipeline on Monday and Friday, you get exactly the same outputs (assuming data and code haven't changed).</p>

  <h3>How DVC Tracks Pipeline Dependencies</h3>

  <p>DVC creates a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of your pipeline. Each node is a stage, each edge is a dependency. When you modify data or code, DVC detects which stages are affected and only reruns those. This is called incremental computation, and it saves hours in large ML projects.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># dvc.yaml - Define your ML pipeline</span>
<span class="va">stages</span>:
  <span class="va">prepare</span>:
    <span class="va">cmd</span>: <span class="st">python scripts/prepare.py</span>
    <span class="va">deps</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">data/raw/customers.csv</span>
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">scripts/prepare.py</span>
    <span class="va">outs</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">data/processed/customers.csv</span>

  <span class="va">featurize</span>:
    <span class="va">cmd</span>: <span class="st">python scripts/featurize.py</span>
    <span class="va">deps</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">data/processed/customers.csv</span>
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">scripts/featurize.py</span>
    <span class="va">outs</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">data/features/features.csv</span>

  <span class="va">train</span>:
    <span class="va">cmd</span>: <span class="st">python scripts/train.py</span>
    <span class="va">deps</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">data/features/features.csv</span>
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">scripts/train.py</span>
    <span class="va">outs</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">models/model.pkl</span>
    <span class="va">metrics</span>:
      <span class="op">-</span> <span class="st">metrics.json</span>:
          <span class="va">cache</span>: <span class="kw">false</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define three pipeline stages. The <code>prepare</code> stage depends on raw data and a script; it outputs processed data. The <code>featurize</code> stage depends on processed data; it outputs features. The <code>train</code> stage depends on features; it outputs a model and metrics. DVC tracks these dependencies and can reproduce the pipeline or any subset of it.</p>
  </div>

