<!-- ===== SECTION 5: EXERCISES ===== -->
<section class="section reveal" id="exercises">
  <span class="section-label">Practice</span>
  <h2>Hands-On Exercises</h2>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 1: Initialize DVC and Track Data</h4>
    <p>Create a new directory, initialize Git and DVC. Create a sample CSV file (100MB or larger). Configure a local remote backend. Use <code>dvc add</code> to track the file. View the generated .dvc file and commit it to Git. Push data to the remote and verify file size in your Git repository vs the remote backend.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 2: Build a Three-Stage Pipeline</h4>
    <p>Define a dvc.yaml with three stages: (1) load raw CSV, (2) clean and filter data, (3) aggregate statistics. Create scripts for each stage. Run <code>dvc repro</code> and verify all stages execute. Modify one script slightly and run <code>dvc repro</code> again, observing that only affected stages recompute.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 3: Reproduce Across Machines</h4>
    <p>Push your DVC-tracked project to GitHub with code, pipeline definition, and .dvc metadata files. Clone the repository on a different machine. Run <code>dvc pull</code> to download data, then <code>dvc repro</code> to reproduce the pipeline. Verify outputs are identical to the original machine.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 4: Track Metrics and Compare Versions</h4>
    <p>Modify one of your pipeline scripts to output metrics (accuracy, loss, F1-score) to metrics.json. Update dvc.yaml to track metrics. Run <code>dvc repro</code> multiple times with different input data or parameters. Use <code>dvc metrics diff</code> to compare metrics across versions. Visualize how changes affect model performance.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <h4>🔬 Exercise 5: Investigate Data Lineage</h4>
    <p>For a DVC-tracked project, use <code>dvc dag</code> to visualize the pipeline. Use <code>dvc status</code> to check what would change if you modified data. Trace a single output (e.g., a trained model) back through the pipeline to its raw data sources. Document the complete lineage: which files depend on which inputs.</p>
  </div>
</section>

